<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gongyi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gongyi is a county-level city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengzhou">Zhengzhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>. Known in antiquity as Gong, it was the seat of the Duchy of Eastern Zhou from 367 BCE until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> annexed it in 249 BCE. It contains the tombs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Northern Song</a> emperors and is the birthplace of the poet Du Fu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gongyi.jpg" alt="Gongyi" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Windmemories · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20240914_Zhengzhou-Xi%27an_HSR_in_Gongyi_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
